# Mutual Non-Disclosure Agreement — N-5 Term and survival

> Module NdaSurvival

This module answers one question: until when do the N-3 obligations of
confidence bind? Nothing else in NDA-MUT is computable, and that is the
correct outcome rather than a gap — N-1 to N-4 and N-6 to N-9 are qualitative
throughout and belong to the vector store.

The answer has **two regimes, and they must not be blended** — three, counting
the period before the Agreement takes effect at all, on which N-5.1 is decisive
and nothing under N-3 binds (CE-0030). While the
Agreement is in force the N-3 obligations bind of their own force: N-3.1 and
N-3.2 are unqualified by anything in N-5, and N-5.1 fixes how long the
Agreement runs. Only once the Agreement has expired or been terminated does
N-5.2 begin to measure anything, and N-5.3 and N-5.4 — drafted expressly "by
way of exception to N-5.2" — can therefore operate on nothing but that
post-expiry tail. Collapsing the two regimes into one is the defect recorded
as CE-0017 and CE-0018; the encoding below keeps them as separate base cases
of `obligations_subsist`, with the whole N-5.3/N-5.4 exception structure
hanging off the tail case alone.

N-5.1 fixes the end of the Agreement and N-5.2 measures the survival period
**from that end**, so the two compose: early termination on 30 days' notice
moves the survival end date *earlier*, never later. N-5.3 and N-5.4 are
sibling exceptions to N-5.2, each displacing the 3-year arithmetic for the
class of information it covers.

`survival_end_date` is an `optional of date`. `Absent` means **no expiry date
is produced**, which is the only honest answer where N-5.3 governs: the
terminus of a trade-secret obligation is a future legal characterisation, not
a date. The companion output `obligations_subsist` answers, as at an
`assessment_date`, whether the obligations are still on foot — that is where
the judgement input `information_remains_trade_secret` does its work.

## Prologue — declarations

`information_is_trade_secret` and `information_remains_trade_secret` are the
two **judgement inputs** of N-5.3 (see `triage/decisions.yaml`). Whether
information "constitutes a trade secret" is a legal characterisation the
Agreement does not define and which must be re-made over time; this module
therefore never decides it, and never asserts an expiry date for information
so characterised.

By deliberate contrast, `is_personal_data` and `date_ceased_to_hold` are
**ordinary inputs**: the class of the information is recorded in the
processing records and continued holding is an inventory fact. N-5.4 is RULE,
not HYBRID, and the contrast with N-5.3 is load-bearing.

`date_ceased_to_hold` is `Absent` while the Recipient still holds the personal
data, and `Present content d` where the data inventory records that it ceased
to hold it on `d`. `termination_notice_date` is `Absent` where no party has
served notice under N-5.1.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope SurvivalEnd:
  input effective_date content date
  input termination_notice_date content optional of date
  input is_personal_data content boolean
  input date_ceased_to_hold content optional of date
  input information_is_trade_secret content boolean
  input information_remains_trade_secret content boolean
  input assessment_date content date
  output agreement_end_date content date
  output survival_end_date content optional of date
  output obligations_subsist content boolean
```

| NO-CLAUSE: the rounding mode is a compiler directive, not a rule. Catala
| date addition raises on an ambiguous result, so the scope must declare one.
| `date round up` is chosen because N-5.1 and N-5.2 confer periods of
| protection — the Agreement "continues for a period of 2 years" and the
| obligations "continue for a period of 3 years" — so where a calendar month
| has no corresponding day (an Effective Date of 29 February) the period is
| taken to the next existing day rather than ending a day early. Rounding
| down would curtail an obligation of confidence by a calendar artefact.
|
| The two assertions are structural well-formedness invariants, not rules of
| the Agreement, and both are consequences of words already quoted below.
| The Agreement "takes effect on the Effective Date", so it cannot end
| before it begins — the invariant CE-0019 broke. And the N-3 obligations
| "survive expiry or termination", which presupposes that they are still
| running at that moment, so a produced confidentiality end date can never
| precede the end of the Agreement — the invariant CE-0017 broke. They are
| asserted rather than trusted so that any future limb which violates them
| fails loudly at the point of computation instead of silently returning an
| impossible date.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  date round up
  assertion agreement_end_date >= effective_date
  assertion
    (match survival_end_date with pattern
     -- Absent: true
     -- Present content ends: ends >= agreement_end_date)
```

## N-3 Obligations of the Recipient

| NDA-MUT N-3.1 (005-mutual-nda.md:45)
|
| The Recipient will keep the Confidential Information confidential and will
| not disclose it to any person except as permitted by this Agreement.

| NDA-MUT N-3.2 (005-mutual-nda.md:48)
|
| The Recipient will use the Confidential Information solely for the
| Permitted Purpose.

**CE-0017 and CE-0018.** These two clauses impose the obligations
unconditionally, and nothing in N-5 qualifies them while the Agreement is on
foot: N-5.2 speaks only to what happens after "expiry or termination", and
N-5.3 and N-5.4 are exceptions to N-5.2 and so reach no further than it
does. So while the Agreement is in force — which N-5.1 determines, and
`agreement_end_date` records — the obligations subsist, whatever has become
of the information's trade-secret status and whether or not the Recipient
still holds the copy it was given. N-6 confirms the direction of travel:
N-6.1 has the Recipient return or destroy the information *during* the life
of the Agreement, and N-6.2 keeps any lawfully retained copy "subject to this
Agreement", so the Agreement contemplates a Recipient that holds nothing and
is still bound. Non-possession is nowhere a discharge.

This is the in-term base case of `obligations_subsist`. No exception attaches
to it, because no clause of the Agreement makes one; and it is bounded at both
ends, the upper bound handing over to the N-5.2 case below and the lower bound
being the subject of the next block.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n3_in_term definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      not (
      assessment_date >= effective_date
      and assessment_date <= agreement_end_date
      )
    consequence equals true
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.1 (005-mutual-nda.md:71)
|
| This Agreement takes effect on the Effective Date and continues for a
| period of 2 years, unless terminated earlier by either party on 30 days'
| written notice.

**CE-0030.** The in-term limb had an upper bound and no lower one: it answered
`true` under the single condition `assessment_date <= agreement_end_date`, and
so said the N-3 obligations subsisted on every date in history before the
Effective Date — including dates before the parties had met. N-5.1 fixes the
other end of the term in terms: the Agreement "takes effect on the Effective
Date", and the obligations in N-3.1 and N-3.2 are obligations of the Recipient
*under this Agreement*, so before that date there is no Agreement to impose
them and nothing in N-3 subsists.

The question is a live one rather than a degenerate one, which is why the limb
needs the bound rather than an assertion. N-2.1 defines Confidential
Information as information disclosed "whether before or after the date of this
Agreement", so a dispute about information the Counterparty disclosed eighteen
months before the Effective Date is squarely within the Agreement's subject
matter and the question "did the N-3 obligations subsist then?" is properly
put. N-2.1 extends the subject matter of the obligations backwards; it does not
backdate the obligations themselves — which is the very reason the parties need
N-2.1 at all. The answer for a pre-commencement date is therefore that they did
not subsist, and it is that answer the limb below gives.

That makes three base cases over `obligations_subsist`, whose conditions
partition the timeline: before the Effective Date, within the term, and after
it. They cannot overlap, because the scope asserts `agreement_end_date >=
effective_date`, and they cannot leave a gap, so exactly one applies at any
assessment date and the whole N-5.3/N-5.4 exception structure still hangs off
the post-expiry case alone (CE-0017, CE-0018).

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_1_before_effect definition obligations_subsist
    under condition assessment_date < effective_date
    consequence equals false
```

## N-5 Term and survival

| NDA-MUT N-5.1 (005-mutual-nda.md:71)
|
| This Agreement takes effect on the Effective Date and continues for a
| period of 2 years, unless terminated earlier by either party on 30 days'
| written notice.

The clause states a term and one exception to it. The exception is encoded as
a Catala exception rather than folded into a conditional, because "unless
terminated earlier" is exactly that.

Notice does not automatically end the Agreement. It ends it only where two
things hold. First, the 30 days must expire **before** the second
anniversary, which is what "terminated earlier" requires; notice served
within the last 30 days of the term therefore leaves the ordinary expiry
undisturbed. Second — **CE-0019** — the notice must be served on or after the
Effective Date. The right to terminate on 30 days' notice is conferred by
this Agreement on "either party" to it, and it does not exist before the
Agreement takes effect, so a notice served during negotiations bites on
nothing. "Earlier" in N-5.1 means earlier than the 2-year expiry, not
earlier than commencement, and a term that ended before it began is excluded
by the clause on its face.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_1_term definition agreement_end_date
    equals effective_date + 2 year

  label n5_1_notice exception n5_1_term definition agreement_end_date
    under condition
      (match termination_notice_date with pattern
       -- Absent: false
       -- Present content notice:
            notice >= effective_date
            and notice + 30 day < effective_date + 2 year)
    consequence equals
      (match termination_notice_date with pattern
       -- Absent: effective_date + 2 year
       -- Present content notice: notice + 30 day)
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.2 (005-mutual-nda.md:75)
|
| The obligations of confidence in N-3 survive expiry or termination of this
| Agreement and continue for a period of 3 years from the date of expiry or
| termination.

The 3 years run from `agreement_end_date`, which is the date of expiry *or*
termination as N-5.1 determines it. This is the composition point: an early
termination under N-5.1 pulls the survival end date back with it.

The clause governs the post-expiry tail and nothing else, so the second base
case of `obligations_subsist` carries the condition `assessment_date >
agreement_end_date`. That condition is what confines N-5.2 — and with it
N-5.3 and N-5.4, which are exceptions to it and must each repeat it — to the
period after the Agreement has gone (CE-0017, CE-0018).

The end date is the last day on which the obligations bind, so
`obligations_subsist` compares with `<=`. This is the base case of the
hierarchy; N-5.3 and N-5.4 are exceptions to it.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    equals Present content (agreement_end_date + 3 year)

  label n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition assessment_date > agreement_end_date
    consequence equals assessment_date <= agreement_end_date + 3 year
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.3 (005-mutual-nda.md:79)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| Confidential Information which constitutes a trade secret continue for so
| long as that information remains a trade secret.

**HYBRID.** Given the judgement that the information constitutes a trade
secret, the deterministic consequence is that the N-5.2 arithmetic is
disapplied and **no expiry date is produced**. Returning a 3-year date here
would be the module confidently asserting the one answer the clause
contradicts; returning an invented indefinite date would be worse. `Absent`
is the encoding of "this clause fixes no date".

Whether the obligations are still on foot is a separate question, and it is
the second judgement input that answers it: after expiry they subsist for so
long as the information remains a trade secret. The lapse of that status is
not a discharge during the term (CE-0018): the exception repeats its
parent's condition `assessment_date > agreement_end_date`, without which it
would cut off an obligation N-5.2 has not yet begun to measure. Where the
status has lapsed after expiry the obligations have ended — but on a date
this module cannot know, so it still produces none.

The condition excludes information that is also personal data, because N-5.4
is a sibling exception to the same base and both would otherwise apply to the
same information. The combined case is dealt with expressly below.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_3 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and not is_personal_data
    consequence equals Absent

  label n5_3_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      assessment_date > agreement_end_date
      and information_is_trade_secret
      and not is_personal_data
    consequence equals information_remains_trade_secret
```

| NDA-MUT N-5.4 (005-mutual-nda.md:83)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| personal data continue for so long as the Recipient holds that personal
| data.

**RULE, not HYBRID.** Both elements are records rather than judgements, so
the end date is computable: it is the date the Recipient ceased to hold the
personal data. While it still holds it, no end date has yet arisen, so
`Absent` is returned for that reason and not N-5.3's.

Note that N-5.4 can shorten as well as lengthen the survival tail: personal
data returned or destroyed under N-6.1 one year after termination ends the
obligation then, well before the N-5.2 three years. What it cannot do is
reach back inside the term (CE-0017). Ceasing to hold the data while the
Agreement is in force exhausts the N-5.4 tail before it starts, so the
obligations run to the end of the Agreement and stop there — hence
`Date.max of agreement_end_date, ceased`, which is also what makes the
"survive" invariant hold. The subsistence limb repeats the parent's
condition `assessment_date > agreement_end_date` for the same reason, and
then answers on the same maximum, so the two outputs cannot disagree.

The class conditions exclude the trade-secret case, for the reason given
under N-5.3.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_4 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      is_personal_data and not information_is_trade_secret
    consequence equals
      (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
       -- Absent: Absent
       -- Present content ceased:
            Present content (Date.max of agreement_end_date, ceased))

  label n5_4_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      assessment_date > agreement_end_date
      and is_personal_data
      and not information_is_trade_secret
    consequence equals
      (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
       -- Absent: true
       -- Present content ceased:
            assessment_date <= (Date.max of agreement_end_date, ceased))
```

## N-5.3 and N-5.4 applying to the same information

| NDA-MUT N-5.3 (005-mutual-nda.md:79)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| Confidential Information which constitutes a trade secret continue for so
| long as that information remains a trade secret.

| NDA-MUT N-5.4 (005-mutual-nda.md:83)
|
| By way of exception to N-5.2, the obligations of confidence in respect of
| personal data continue for so long as the Recipient holds that personal
| data.

Information can be both a trade secret and personal data, and then both
exceptions bear on it. The Agreement states no precedence between them, so
this block encodes the interaction rather than either clause alone — hence
the two quotations.

Neither clause disapplies the other: each independently continues the N-3
obligations for its own period. So the obligations subsist while **either**
period runs. As to the end date, the two limbs are not symmetrical. While the
information remains a trade secret the N-5.3 limb is running and fixes no
date, so none is produced. Once that status has lapsed, the only period left
running is N-5.4's, whose terminus is a recorded fact — so where the
inventory records the date holding ceased, that date (floored at the end of
the Agreement) is the answer, and continuing to return `Absent` would assert
indefiniteness on the strength of a limb that has stopped running. Where
holding has not ceased, no end date has arisen under either limb and `Absent`
is right for N-5.4's own reason.

Writing this limb out is what keeps the two sibling exceptions mutually
exclusive. Left overlapping they would both apply and Catala would raise
`Conflict` at runtime — a defect the type checker does not catch. Like its
siblings it repeats the parent's `assessment_date > agreement_end_date`, so
neither ground can discharge the obligations during the term.

```catala
scope SurvivalEnd:
  label n5_34 exception n5_2 definition survival_end_date
    under condition
      information_is_trade_secret and is_personal_data
    consequence equals
      (if information_remains_trade_secret then Absent
       else
         (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
          -- Absent: Absent
          -- Present content ceased:
               Present content (Date.max of agreement_end_date, ceased)))

  label n5_34_subsist exception n5_2_subsist definition obligations_subsist
    under condition
      assessment_date > agreement_end_date
      and information_is_trade_secret
      and is_personal_data
    consequence equals
      information_remains_trade_secret
      or (match date_ceased_to_hold with pattern
          -- Absent: true
          -- Present content ceased:
               assessment_date <= (Date.max of agreement_end_date, ceased))
```
